%S A392984 -1,1,-1,1,-1,3,-5,-3,-1,3,-7,1,-3,-5,3,-3,-1,3,-3,-9,-1,-1,1,1,-11,1,
%T A392984 -7,-5,-3,5,7,-1,3,-15,3,-5,3,-11,-3,5,9,5,5,1,-5,-3,-19,-7,-13,-7,-5,
%U A392984 1,15,13,-7,-9,-15,1,1,-5,-5,13,3,-15,-11,-1,9,-1,-9,7,-5,5,9,-5,7,-5,-3,13,-17,-9
%N A392984 a(n) = (A_0 - A_2)/p, where p = A007521(n), A_0 is the sum of the quartic residues mod p, and A_2 is the sum of the quadratic non-quartic residues mod p.
%C A392984 Write k(p) = (A_0 - A_2)/p for the value at the prime p, so that a(n) = k(p) with p = A007521(n). For a prime p == 1 (mod 4) the group (Z/pZ)* is the disjoint union of four cosets of the subgroup of fourth powers. Coset 0 is the set of quartic residues {m : m^((p-1)/4) == 1 (mod p)}, coset 2 is the set of quadratic non-quartic residues {m : m^((p-1)/4) == -1 (mod p)}, and A_j denotes the sum of the elements of coset j.
%C A392984 The definition is canonical: which elements lie in cosets 0 and 2 does not depend on the choice of primitive root. Only cosets 1 and 3 are interchanged under the ambiguity chi_4 <-> conjugate(chi_4).
%C A392984 For p == 1 (mod 8) the quartic character chi_4 is even, so B_{1,chi_4} = 0 and A_0 - A_2 = 0 for every such prime. The domain is therefore restricted to p == 5 (mod 8), where chi_4 is odd and k(p) is a nonzero odd integer.
%C A392984 By the classical identity L(0,chi) = -B_{1,chi} (Leopoldt, 1958) together with the Carlitz-Barsky integrality theorem, k(p) = -Re L(0,chi_4), where chi_4 is a quartic Dirichlet character mod p. Since chi_4 is odd for p == 5 (mod 8), L(0,chi_4) is a Gaussian integer, so k(p) is a rational integer.
%C A392984 k(p) is odd for every prime in the domain. This follows from the (1+i)-adic valuation of L(0,chi_4) being exactly 1 for p == 5 (mod 8), p >= 13.
%C A392984 The prime p = 5 = A007521(1) is excluded because k(5) = -3/5 is not an integer, the unique exception in this congruence class. This is why the offset is 2.
%C A392984 |k(p)| << sqrt(p)*log(p) by the Polya-Vinogradov inequality. In practice k(p) is much smaller. Over the 19622 primes p == 5 (mod 8) below 10^6, the largest value of |k(p)| is 779, at p = 953941, and the largest value of |k(p)|/sqrt(p) is 0.8274..., at p = 661621.
%C A392984 Let p be an odd prime not of the form 2^a + 1, put a = v_2(p-1), and let H be the group of 2^a-th powers mod p. Then K(p) = 2*(Sum_{x in H} x)/p - (p-1)/2^a is an integer independent of the choice of character of order 2^a mod p. The case a = 2 is k(p); the case a = 1 gives K(p) = -h(-p) for p == 3 (mod 4), the class numbers of A002143.
%H A392984 Joseph Farland, <a href="/A392984/b392984.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..19623</a>
%H A392984 K. Girstmair, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1993-1195428-3">The relative class numbers of imaginary cyclic fields of degrees 4, 6, 8, and 10</a>, Math. Comp. 61 (1993), 881-887.
%H A392984 K. Girstmair, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02940820">Class number factors and distribution of residues</a>, Abh. Math. Sem. Univ. Hamburg 67 (1997), 65-104.
%H A392984 R. H. Hudson, <a href="https://doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1984.115.129">Class numbers of imaginary cyclic quartic fields and related quaternary systems</a>, Pacific J. Math. 115 (1984), 129-142.
%H A392984 R. H. Hudson and K. S. Williams, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1982-0669664-9">Class number formulae of Dirichlet type</a>, Math. Comp. 39 (1982), 725-732.
%H A392984 B. Setzer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1980-0583516-2">The determination of all imaginary, quartic, abelian number fields with class number 1</a>, Math. Comp. 35 (1980), 1383-1386.
%F A392984 k(p) = (A_0 - A_2)/p, where A_0 = Sum_{m = 1..p-1, m^((p-1)/4) == 1 (mod p)} m and A_2 = Sum_{m = 1..p-1, m^((p-1)/4) == p-1 (mod p)} m.
%F A392984 k(p) = 2*A_0/p - (p-1)/4, since A_0 + A_2 = p*(p-1)/4.
%F A392984 k(p) = (1/(2*p))*Sum_{x=1..p-1} (x^4 mod p) - (p-1)/4, since x -> x^4 mod p is four-to-one onto coset 0.
%F A392984 k(p) = -Re L(0,chi_4). Since L(0,chi_4) = -B_{1,chi_4} by the Leopoldt formula, and B_{1,chi_4} = (1/p)*Sum_{m=1..p-1} chi_4(m)*m, this gives k(p) = Re B_{1,chi_4}.
%F A392984 k(p)^2 + t(p)^2 = 2*h*(p), where t(p) = (A_1 - A_3)/p and h*(p) is the relative class number of the imaginary cyclic quartic field of conductor p (Hudson and Williams, 1982; Hudson, 1984, equation (2.3)).
%e A392984 p = 13 = A007521(2): coset 0 is {1, 3, 9}, since 1^3 = 1, 3^3 = 27 == 1 and 9^3 = 729 == 1 (mod 13); coset 2 is {4, 10, 12}, since 4^3 == 10^3 == 12^3 == -1 (mod 13). So A_0 = 13, A_2 = 26 and k(13) = (13 - 26)/13 = -1, giving a(2) = -1.
%e A392984 p = 29 = A007521(3): A_0 = 116, A_2 = 87, k(29) = 29/29 = 1, giving a(3) = 1.
%e A392984 p = 101 = A007521(7): A_0 = 1414, A_2 = 1111, k(101) = 303/101 = 3, giving a(7) = 3.
%e A392984 p = 109 = A007521(8): A_0 = 1199, A_2 = 1744, k(109) = -545/109 = -5, giving a(8) = -5.
%t A392984 kInvariant[p_] := Module[{e = (p-1)/4, A0 = 0, A2 = 0}, Do[Which[PowerMod[n, e, p] == 1, A0 += n, PowerMod[n, e, p] == p-1, A2 += n], {n, 1, p-1}]; (A0 - A2)/p];
%t A392984 Select[Prime[Range[400]], Mod[#, 8] == 5 && # >= 13 &] // Map[kInvariant]
%o A392984 (Python)
%o A392984 from sympy import primerange
%o A392984 def k(p):
%o A392984     e = (p - 1) // 4
%o A392984     A0 = sum(n for n in range(1, p) if pow(n, e, p) == 1)
%o A392984     A2 = sum(n for n in range(1, p) if pow(n, e, p) == p - 1)
%o A392984     return (A0 - A2) // p
%o A392984 print([k(p) for p in primerange(13, 3000) if p % 8 == 5])
%o A392984 (PARI) k(p) = sum(x=1, p-1, lift(Mod(x,p)^4))/(2*p) - (p-1)/4;
%o A392984 forprime(p=13, 3000, if(p%8==5, print1(k(p), ", ")))
